# Introduction

<p class="lead">This is where the <strong>Llama Brand</strong>, <strong>Llama UI</strong> and <strong>Llama Chat</strong> are designed, built and checked. Everything in it is the real component, font or file, so what you see here is what ships.</p>

## In short

The **Llama Brand** (the icon, Llama Sans and Llama Mono, the colours), the component library **Llama UI** built on it, the app **Llama Chat** built from that, and the communications made with them, in one place, for the people and agents who design and build Llama products.

<Figure n={1} caption="The building blocks, from the bottom: the Llama Brand, Llama UI built on it and Llama Chat built from that. The communications are made from the brand and the app.">
	<Stack />
</Figure>

## What it's for

<Figure n={2} caption="Four uses. The handover runs with npm run export:design-system.">
	<Uses />
</Figure>

## How we think

The short version of **Philosophy**; the reasons are in **Ideology**, **Product** and **Design**.

<Figure n={3} caption="Five ideas, each drawn with the real system: the devices a model runs on, the first run's recommendation, the three places the character lives, a control on its grid and one token chain.">
	<Principles />
</Figure>

## How it fits together

<Figure n={4} caption="The products and their repositories. llama.brand supplies the brand; llama.cpp holds the engine and Llama UI, in tools/ui, this Storybook's home; the native apps run llama-server and open Llama Chat. llama.app shows models the way Llama Chat does.">
	<Products />
</Figure>

Inside Llama UI, the tokens go from primitives to semantic roles to components, in `src/lib/styles/tokens/`, and `DESIGN.md` is generated from them. Llama UI draws the interface and handles interaction; each app owns its own state.

